(Q9) A file needs to be accessible by multiple users but only writable by the owner. How will you set permissions?
To make a file accessible by multiple users but only writable by the owner, you need to set permissions that give:
    • Owner: Read + Write permissions 
    • Group/Others: Read-only permissions 

Code:

bash
# Set exact permissions
chmod u=rw,g=r,o=r filename.txt

# Or use 'a' for all (group + others)
chmod u=rw,a=r filename.txt

# Step by step approach
chmod u+rw filename.txt    # Give owner read+write
chmod go+r filename.txt    # Give group and others read
chmod go-wx filename.txt   # Remove write and execute for group/others
This is how you set permissions for the user and owner.
(Q10)  How do you check the manual page for any Linux commands?
Syntax:
bash
man [options] command_name
Code:
bash
# View manual for ls command
man ls
# View manual for chmod command
man chmod

# View manual for grep command
man grep

# View manual for man command itself
man man
Every manual page typically contains:
    • NAME - Command name and brief description 
    • SYNOPSIS - Command syntax 
    • DESCRIPTION - Detailed explanation 
    • OPTIONS - Available command options 
    • EXAMPLES - Usage examples 
    • FILES - Related files 
    • SEE ALSO - Related commands 
    • AUTHOR - Who wrote the command




